<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- CasaEnComú nightly backup — docs/SCAFFOLD.md § 8.4. Install: cp to ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ then
	     launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.kenobi.casaencomu-backup.plist
	     It goes through `ssh localhost`, and both halves of that are load-bearing (measured, #21):
	       - launchd's own process may reach Docker but may NOT write into iCloud Drive. It answers
	         EINTR, "Interrupted system call", which reads like a hardware fault and is not one.
	       - an ssh session may write iCloud but may NOT read /Volumes, where the data lives.
	     So the script does every read inside the container, which has the disk bind-mounted, and only
	     the encrypted stream crosses out — and that write happens in the ssh session, which is allowed.
	     `-F /dev/null` because ~/.ssh/config includes a colima file on /Volumes: ssh dies parsing it
	     before it ever tries to connect. Nothing on this Mac has Full Disk Access; granting it to sshd
	     would remove the first constraint but not this file, so it is not the fix.
	     launchd does not expand ~, so /bin/sh resolves $HOME for the log; the remote shell expands ~. -->
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.kenobi.casaencomu-backup</string>
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/bin/sh</string>
		<string>-c</string>
		<string>exec /usr/bin/ssh -F /dev/null -o BatchMode=yes -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no localhost '~/.local/bin/casaencomu-backup.sh' >>"$HOME/Library/Logs/casaencomu-backup.log" 2>&amp;1</string>
	</array>
	<key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
	<dict>
		<key>Hour</key>
		<integer>3</integer>
		<key>Minute</key>
		<integer>30</integer>
	</dict>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
